\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Turkey Moves To Halt Lira's Fall\b0\par\par\uc2 By Marc Champion And Anita Greil   \par \uc2 983 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2011\par 22:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly this week to stop an abrupt slide in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even as foreign corporate investors bet on the economy as a bright spot amid the global doldrums.\par \par \uc2 The unusual paradox illustrates complex strains in the world economy, as developing nations have had to struggle with sharp ups and downs in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , threatening growth even as they are counted on to shore up struggling developed economies.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent $350 million in an auction to purchase Turkish lira Friday, bringing the week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  total to $1.64 billion. At the same time, the bank has been cutting reserve requirements for commercial banks, pumping liquidity into the financial system.\par \par \uc2 In striking contrast, two U.S.-based private-equity funds this week bet about $1 billion on Turkey for the longer term.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's economy grew by more than 10% in the first half of the year. It has a strong banking sector, and enjoys low public debt and budget-deficit levels that neighboring European Union economies can envy.\par \par \uc2 But concerns over the falling lira are the latest in a series of worries some economists have expressed over what lies around the corner for this country of 74 million.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's moves this week effectively reversed the unorthodox policies it has followed since late last year. The bank cut interest rates despite double-digit economic growth, as it sought to deter volatile speculative inflows that were then flooding emerging markets, and to drive down the value of the lira to boost exports and cut imports. At the same time, it raised reserve requirements to deter bank lending and temper demand.\par \par \uc2 Now the bank worries that a global downturn--particularly in the European Union, Turkey's dominant trade partner--could take Turkey's booming economy down with it. A too-rapid fall in the lira could stoke inflation, already well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 5.5% target, and trigger a rush to the exits by short-term foreign investment that finances much of the country's massive current-account deficit, economists say.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's primary concern isn't the value of the lira, but inflation," said Mustafa Akcay, at \uc2 Moody's\uc2  economic research, in West Chester, Pa. "The lira dropped around 10% in September alone, and this rapid decline creates inflation."\par \par \uc2 A core concern remains the size of Turkey's current-account deficit--now above 9% of gross domestic product--and the short-term nature of the foreign-investment flows that finance it. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is increasingly concerned that a disorderly sell-off in the lira could worsen capital outflows, as investors see the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a one-way bet," said a research note Thursday by Capital Economics, a London-based consultancy.\par \par \uc2 As the global economy darkens, Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has turned to foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions to stabilize the lira, rather than raising interest rates, which could damp growth.\par \par \uc2 The IMF in September advised the opposite, saying the bank should raise rates and conserve its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The reserves provide a cushion against any sudden flight of the short-term investment flows, should Turkey have to cover the current-account deficit.\par \par \uc2 "We don't waste our reserves...but they are not for decoration on our balance sheet," said the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deputy governor, Ibrahim Turhan, interviewed Thursday at the Global Economic Symposium in Kiel, Germany. As of Oct. 4, the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at $85 billion.\par \par \uc2 The lira was trading at 1.85 to the dollar Friday, down from a peak of just over 1.90 earlier in the week.\par \par \uc2 The IMF forecasts that Turkey will grow by 2.5% next year, a sharp slowdown from current heady levels, but well above forecasts for most developed economies. And longer term, rising inflows of foreign investment this year suggest investors are willing to overlook Turkey's short-term risks.\par \par \uc2 Foreign direct investment in the seven months to July was $9.1 billion, according to Turkey's economy ministry, the same total as for all 12 months of last year. This week saw the launch of a new $1 billion fund by one U.S.-based private equity fund, \uc2 Cerberus Capital Management LP\uc2 , and the unveiling of a $300 million venture in a Turkish private-school business by another, \uc2 Carlyle Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Investors and Turkish officials point to the country's youthful population. Its median age is 28, compared with 44 for Germany and 37 for the U.S., according to United Nations data. They say that explains their optimism for longer-term growth. Meanwhile, a high-profile foreign policy and robust growth rates over the past year have drawn attention.\par \par \uc2 "It didn't take us a week to get 30 companies to come with us," on a trip to promote investment ties between the U.S. and Turkey, said Thomas J. Donohue, president and chief executive of the U.S. Chamber of Commerce, interviewed in Istanbul on Wednesday. He said those companies included \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  and \uc2 General Electric\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Look what's happening in Europe, in the Middle East," he said, contrasting the debt crisis in Greece and turmoil in Libya with Turkey's relative stability and strong growth.\par \par \uc2 Other larger deals this year include U.K.-based \uc2 Diageo PLC\uc2 's (DEO) $2.1 billion purchase of Turkish drinks-maker Mey Icki. In another $2.1 billion deal last month, Jersey-based \uc2 Vallares PLC\uc2  (VLRS.LN), an investment vehicle run by former BP Chief Executive Tony Hayward, merged with Turkey's Genel Energy Investment Ltd. Genel has significant oil production in Northern Iraq.\par \par \uc2 -By Marc Champion at marc.champion@wsj.com and Anita Greil at anita.greil@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Todd Buell in Kiel, Germany, contributed to this article. [ 10-07-11 1614ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111007e7a7000qf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
