\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Turkey \highlight\uc2  May  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Market If Lira Weakness Intensifies\b0\par\par\uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2011\par 19:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if lira weakness intensifies\par \par \uc2 -- Measures taken so far to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sliding have had limited success\par \par \uc2 -- But some analysts say the bank isn't ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  just yet\par \par \uc2 By Eva Szalay and Yeliz Candemir\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 The Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may sell dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stop the lira depreciating further if fears of a global slowdown spook investors out of the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Fears of a direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have grown after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Erdem Basci said Friday that further lira weakness would be unwelcome. The lira has slipped almost 9% against the dollar in September, which hit a fresh record high against the lira at TRY1.8732 Sept. 28.\par \par \uc2 "You keep reserves exactly for these type of situations and if fears of a double-dip recession intensify, they won't think twice to use it," said Sengul Dagdeviren, chief economist at ING Bank Turkey in Istanbul.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already sold around $3 billion, approximately 3% of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $87 billion, at its weekly auctions to shore up the lira.\par \par \uc2 So far, attempts to stall the lira's decline seem to have had little success.\par \par \uc2 Europe's emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have faced increasing pressure in recent weeks as fears of a global slowdown intensified and spooked investors out of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the lira, the Polish zloty and the Hungarian forint. Polish state-owned Bank Gospodarstwa Krajowego stepped into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the second time in a week Thursday to support the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars against the zloty.\par \par \uc2 "The risk for the lira is that capital outflows might continue if risk aversion remains strong but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain ready to take steps to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Dagdeviren said. "The question is how significant the outflows will be."\par \par \uc2 Turkey recovered remarkably from the recession last year, when its economy expanded 8.9% after having shrunk 4.7% in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Fueling Turkey's red-hot economic growth has been rampant consumer demand, financed by bank loans and credit cards. This has left the country with a big trade deficit because imports are rising faster than exports. Moreover, the rapid, import-led growth led to a yawning current-account deficit. This makes Turkey vulnerable to swings in the mood of international investors, who won't hesitate to withdraw cash once they grow wary of Turkey's ability to withstand the global downturn.\par \par \uc2 Already the economic outlook has darkened. The International Monetary Fund recently lowered its growth forecast for Turkey to just 6.6% in 2011 from its earlier 8.7% estimate. For 2012, the IMF predicts growth to slow down to just 2.2%.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will refrain from taking drastic steps for now.\par \par \uc2 "The lira is cheap already and close to the bottom, said Benoit Anne, head of emerging-market strategy at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  in London, adding that "no spectacular measures need to be undertaken at this stage."\par \par \uc2 "The depreciation of the lira is part of the soft landing," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Basci said Friday.\par \par \uc2 But some economists disagreed. Garanti Yatirim analyst Tufan Comert, for example, said no hard data support Basci's argument, and a hard landing could still come.\par \par \uc2 Already, lira depreciation is stoking investor concerns over the country's inflation, which Basci said may exceed the 5.5% target in 2011.\par \par \uc2 -By Yeliz Candemir and Eva Szalay, Dow Jones Newswires; yeliz.candemir@dowjones.com and eva.szalay@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 30, 2011 13:27 ET (17:27 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110930e79u0055r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
