\par\par \b \uc2 Turkey's Unorthodox Policy Potential Template For Tackling Emerging-Market Dilemma\b0\par\par\uc2 1,261 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Februar 2011\par 22:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 - While other countries have raised rates, Turkey went against orthodox policy and cut them\par \par \uc2 - Instead, it used reserve requirements to manage inflation and now other countries are copying\par \par \uc2 - Promisingly, both the lira and prices have moderated, but it's a risky strategy\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     By Cynthia Lin \par     Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Last year, while other emerging-market central banks felt the frustration of seeing their interest rate increases drive up local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and spur inflows of volatile "hot money," Turkey's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  opted for a less conventional approach to fighting inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against criticism that it was playing with fire, the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  went the other way: It cut rates, supporting a weaker lira, and instead turned to bank reserve requirements as the primary weapon for containing credit growth and its overheating effects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier Tuesday, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept its benchmark rate unchanged at 6.25%, but that was after two consecutive cuts in as many months that left it at an all-time low in January. Now, one month since that surprising move, there is evidence to suggest the Turkish strategy is working, at least for now.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It could even become a model for other emerging-market central banks that are now dealing with a real conundrum: How to beat inflation without attracting volatile short-term money inflows. Those flows are driving up their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , undermining export competitiveness, and raising the risk of a destabilizing outflow if and when risk sentiment turns negative.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So far this year, the Turkish lira is down more than 3% on the dollar, a possible sign that hot money inflows have died off in response to the lower rates. But while this might otherwise have raised fears of imported inflation, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  survey released last Tuesday showed annual inflation expectations were at 6.56% for 2011, a decline from the previous 6.61% projection. That's likely an outcome of repeated increases in reserve requirements for banks, which since November have gone up from a blanket 5.5% for all deposits to as high as 12% for deposits of immediate access.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Turkish approach is risky, as it challenges the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's credibility, but "the proof is in the pudding," said Neil Shearing, senior emerging-markets economist at Capital Economics, pointing to the weak lira as an early sign the policy is working.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to Shearing, those countries with structurally similar economies could do well to follow Turkey's approach. He suggested Brazil, which despite being a commodity-based economy where Turkey is not, shares a key similarity "in that neither country saves a lot," which tends to bolster interest rates at home. "Given the high level of real interest rates in Brazil, they should have one eye on Turkey definitely," Shearing said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In contrast, however, Turkey has seen subdued inflation readings, providing them a unique window of opportunity to use lower rates to target on hot money issues and its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --a luxury most say will not last for long. "They've been able to (cut rates) because the inflation data has been benign in the past few months so that's given them a window of opportunity to do this," said Paul Biszko, senior emerging-markets analyst at RBC. "Turkey's turn is coming in terms of inflation pressure, but it's not here yet."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the past, Turkey and Brazil were both seen as avid practitioners of inflation-targeting, but as their policies have diverged, this has pulled their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in opposite directions. While Turkey's benchmark rate is now at a record low, Brazil since last year has raised its benchmark rate by 250 basis points to a towering 11.25%. Meanwhile, the real continues to trend stronger against the dollar, despite aggressive dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Turkey\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also pursues this costly tactic of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --as do many emerging-market central banks--but economists say that its $40 million in daily purchases is now aimed more at bulking up its relatively low foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves than reversing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The International Monetary Fund, however, has warned of the extra liquidity caused by these dollar purchases, which are seen as counterproductive to the slew of tools Turkey has deployed to slow credit flows. Showing its discomfort with Turkey's policy framework, the IMF also recently said that a "greater effort" is needed from Turkey's fiscal policy side to make monetary moves more effective.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, other emerging-market countries are warming to the reserve requirement strategy that Turkey pioneered, seeing it as an alternative means of curtailing bank lending and the strong domestic demand its has fostered.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Biszko said that other countries have started following Turkey's lead. Developing nations such as China, Russia, Poland and India have all hiked reserve requirements. Biszko added that reserve adjustments can help cool import demand and take pressure off Turkey's hefty current account deficit, which was recently estimated to hit about 6% of 2010 gross domestic product.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the time being, Turkey's measures appear to have stemmed hot money inflows into bank deposits and money markets without also sparking a destabilizing increase in longer-term redemptions. A net $8 billion in short-term hot-money outflows had occurred between mid-December and mid-January although local bonds still saw a net inflow of about $2.2 billion, according to a report in early February citing Finance Minister Mehmet Simsek.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, Turkey's success in its risky strategy is by no means assured.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The lira's weakness "cuts both ways," said Nick Chamie, global head of emerging-markets research at RBC. Without a stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to eat away at inflation, prices could yet get away from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's control. Chamie notes that reserve requirements alone are too blunt an instrument, and eventually the sharper tool of higher rates will be needed to fight pricing pressures. Also, if policy makers indeed have to backtrack and aggressively raise rates from "behind the curve" to restore their credibility and contain rising prices, it could make for a harsh landing and economic whiplash.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although Turkey's January headline consumer price index showed a dramatic decline to 4.9% year-over-year--a 41-year low--it was mostly held down by an easy base comparison against last year's high levels. Both core inflation and producer price readings came in higher than expected, signaling pricing pressures down the line, especially at a time of rising oil and food prices worldwide.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Did they create trouble down the line? I think no matter what, Turkey in a way is put into a difficult spot," said Christian Keller, chief economist for European emerging markets at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 , "not really due to the policy, but the global commodity price developments working against them."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet for now, Turkey is the envy of other developing countries. The question is whether they will follow its policy model. If they do, and Turkey turns out to have been wrong, the implications for global inflation could be quite negative.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They're making a very calculated gamble," Keller says. "They know that with a weaker lira on top of global commodity price increases, there could be a significant pass through into inflation later in the year, but for now they want to take advantage of the historically low annual inflation prints to lower policy rates further."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Cynthia Lin, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4403; cynthia.lin@dowjones.com [ 15-02-11 2150GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110215e72f001cj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
