\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Turkey's enviable growth outlook revives laggard lira\b0\par\par\uc2 867 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2010\par 15:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Lira seen advancing to 1.40-1.35 to dollar\par \par \uc2 * Lira lagged 2010 gains in Eastern European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  not seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in appreciation\par \par \uc2 * Impending rate hikes could encourage carry trade\par \par \uc2 By Alexandra Hudson\par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, April 28 (Reuters) - Turkey's lira is finally rallying, with economy set to enjoy one of 2010's fastest growth rates, and a tightening policy by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that will help spark demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Turkey's projected growth rate this year of 5.2 percent is faster than the euro zone, emerging Europe and the CIS region, according to the IMF's latest economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 In addition, minimal risk is seen to Turkey's exports from a higher \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "We expect Turkish interest rates to rise by 200 basis points in 2010, and the country will benefit from this increasing interest rate divergence. The euro-zone looks a mess and we don't think it will be before 2011 that the ECB can raise rates," said Justin Patrie, emerging Europe analyst at Business Monitor International.\par \par \uc2 Having already rallied almost 3 percent since the start of April to around 1.48 to the dollar, analysts now think the lira could advance as far as 1.35-1.40, as it catches up on gains made by other Turkish assets, and other emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There is fundamental demand for the lira. Conditions in Turkey are excellent -- certainly much stronger than the United States and the euro zone. Turkey has a strong, healthy credit market, some of Europe's most robust banks and private consumption is a strong recovery story," Patrie added.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen as having plenty of room to appreciate, supported by a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that will allow such moves.\par \par \uc2 The lira gained just 3 percent to the dollar in 2009, after losing almost a quarter of its value the year before. Turkish equities <.XU100> by contrast doubled in value and bonds yields touched record lows on the country's resilience to the crisis, which earned it upgrades by all major credit ratings agencies.\par \par \uc2 Economist Tim Ash at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  said the belief the lira had lagged other emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as more convincing signs of recovery had all helped lift the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Turkish ministers predict first-quarter growth will be in double figures, after 6 percent growth in the fourth-quarter.\par \par \uc2 LAGGARD\par \par \uc2 Despite April's gains, the lira is still only trading 1 percent higher than at the start of 2010, compared with a gain of almost 5 percent for Poland's zloty <EURPLN=>, 3.5 percent for the Czech crown, and 8.4 percent for the rouble.\par \par \uc2 A fresh bout of tension in February between Turkey's Islamist-rooted government and the judiciary and military -- bastions of Turkey's secularist foundations -- briefly hit confidence in Turkish assets, but the row has since eased.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's parliament is discussing contentious reforms to the constitution, intended to bring it into harmony with European-Union norms, but for now the growth story is dominant.\par \par \uc2 "Investors have come to expect an element of political instability in Turkey and as long as it isn't a major shock it shouldn't affect assets," said economist Paul Biszko at \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RBC has upgraded the lira to "overweight" from "marketweight", due to its "valuation, attractive carry, growth upside risks and a non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 INFLATION FEAR\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to tolerate lira strength because the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still trading well below highs of around 1.18 to the dollar in August 2008, and because lira appreciation also will help cap energy inflation by reducing the cost of oil.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  targets inflation of 6.5 percent by the end of 2010 but it is currently running at 9.56 percent. The bank has signalled it will hike rates later this year when it sees recovery less endangered by monetary tightening.\par \par \uc2 Most analysts expect rate hikes of 200 basis points to follow although the bank has already begun withdrawing liquidity.\par \par \uc2 If Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as expected, becomes one of the first in the region to tighten policy rates it will likely spur the carry trade, which all but disappeared after it slashed rates by 10.25 percentage points from late 2008 to 2009.\par \par \uc2 POSSIBLE RISKS\par \par \uc2 Risks to the lira's upwards trend include Turkey's current account deficit, which fell sharply during the recession, but is rising again. February's deficit of $2.6 billion was more than eight times wider than in February 2009.\par \par \uc2 Another factor is the substantial holdings of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Turkish citizens and their tendency to switch from one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to another.\par \par \uc2 A more expensive lira could also irritate the country's large export sector.\par \par \uc2 "A stronger lira could be seen as bad for growth dynamics as it hinders export performance. But more than the value of the lira, demand conditions in Turkey's export markets are more significant in my opinion," said economist Levent Durusoy at \uc2 Yatirim Finansman\uc2 . (Editing by Neil Stempleman)\par \par \uc2 TURKEY-LIRA (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100501e64s00087\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
