\par\par \b \uc2 Q+A-How dangerous is Turkish military plot report?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ibon Villelabeitia   \par \uc2 962 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2009\par 10:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  ANKARA, June 17 (Reuters) - An alleged plot by the Turkish military to destabilise the Islamist-rooted ruling AK Party could pitch the European Union candidate country into political turmoil.\par \par \uc2  The government has taken legal action after a report that the military had drawn up a plan to stop the AK Party and an influential religious movement from "destroying Turkey's secular order and replacing it by an Islamist state". [ID:nLG375319]\par \par \uc2  WHAT DOES THE REPORT ALLEGE?\par \par \uc2  Taraf, a liberal newspaper often critical of the military, published on Friday what it said was a document from the  powerful General Staff office, signed by a senior navy officer.\par \par \uc2  The alleged document, entitled "Plan of Action for Combating Fundamentalism" describes plans to discredit the AK Party through a media campaign, stir up party divisions, foment nationalist opposition to the government and plant weapons in houses used by associates of Muslim preacher Fethullah Gulen to suggest the religious movement is involved in militant activities.\par \par \uc2  It is not the first time reports have surfaced alleging efforts to undermine the Islamist-rooted AK Party government.\par \par \uc2  WHAT CAN HAPPEN NEXT?\par \par \uc2  Whether the document proves to be fake or authentic, the controversy has further strained ties between the AK Party and the staunchly secular military, which is already under pressure over a separate investigation into a suspected right-wing network accused of plotting a coup against the government. A trial has already begun.\par \par \uc2  Generals and the AK Party, which has roots in political Islam but also embraces centre-right and nationalist elements, have long been at odds over the direction of the mainly Muslim country. Turkey has a secular constitution.\par \par \uc2  The story has embarrassed the military and could end up strengthening the government in a long power struggle with hardline secularists over public opinion and Turkey's direction.\par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Tayyip Erdogan on Tuesday urged military and civilian courts to "comfort society" and swiftly conclude an investigation into the report. Armed forces chief General Ilker Basbug has declined to say whether he believed such a secret document existed. On Tuesday the two men met for 80 minutes.\par \par \uc2  The military has launched its own investigation.\par \par \uc2  If the document is genuine, it would fuel accusations by the AK Party that the secular establishment, of which the army is a key pillar, is willing to use anti-democratic means to undermine the political party, which first took power in 2002.\par \par \uc2  Secularists have long accused the AK Party and its conservative supporters of harbouring an Islamist agenda and seeking to turn Turkey away from its Western secular values upon which the republic was founded on by Mustafa Kemal Ataturk. \par \par \uc2  AK, which can look to support in the pro-western middle classes, beyond the country's conservative religious heartland, denies it has any such Islamist agenda.\par \par \uc2  Secularists also are suspicious of Gulen and his followers who they believe have infiltrated key institutions in Turkey, particularly the police.\par \par \uc2  If the row escalates, the government will be dragged into a new confrontation rather than pursuing reforms towards EU membership that help attract more foreign investment.\par \par \uc2  Regardless of which camp prevails, analysts say the price could be high for Turkey's political stability. Domestic turbulence has in the past hampered progress in economic and political reforms aimed at gaining EU membership and have been a source of concern for investors.\par \par \uc2  IS THERE A RISK OF A COUP?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's military has a long history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in politics, including staging several coups by force or through pressure, most recently in 1997 when it helped oust an Islamist government. Few believe there is a risk of another coup, partly because the military would not have public backing.\par \par \uc2  The military has traditionally been very careful to ensure it remains respected by Turks. The military is frequently viewed as the most respected institution in Turkey, according to polls.\par \par \uc2  But recent setbacks for the armed forces and the damage to its reputation could raise the risk that generals may not be able to hold back rebellion among ranks and that rogue elements would resort to clandestine actions in an attempt to sow chaos.\par \par \uc2  Open hostilities between the AK Party and the General Staff, deeply suspicious of each other, have eased since the less confrontational Basbug took command of the NATO army last year.\par \par \uc2  But the arrests of retired generals and active officers over the past two years for their alleged links to the suspected Ergenekon right-wing group and attacks by pro-government media against the once-untouchable military may force Basbug to take a harder public line to calm brewing discontent.\par \par \uc2  The military -- still Turkey's most respected institution according to recent opinion polls -- has taken a back seat in recent years, but a climate of growing government-military confrontation might drag generals into the political arena.\par \par \uc2  ARE MARKETS CONCERNED?\par \par \uc2  Turkey can barely afford more domestic turbulence, with the economy suffering a sharp slowdown. The International Monetary Fund expects Turkey's economy to shrink 5.1 percent this year, after growing 5.9 percent annually between 2002-2008.\par \par \uc2  Traders said markets were for the moment shrugging off the row, but it was expected to pile further pressure on Turkey in coming months. \par \par \uc2  Tensions between the military and the government may add risk to a business environment where the lira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen coming under pressure as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to cut rates to prop up the economy and with no IMF loan programme in place.\par \par \uc2  (Editing by Paul de Bendern and Ralph Boulton)  \par \par \uc2 TURKEY-MILITARY/ (Q&A)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|G|RBN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|RNA|MD|RNP|DNP|PGE|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090617e56h000gh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
