\par\par \b \uc2 PRESS DIGEST - Turkey - Sept 21   \b0\par\par\uc2 375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2007\par 07:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ANKARA, Sept 21 (Reuters) - These are the leading stories in the Turkish press on Friday. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy.   \par \par \uc2 HURRIYET   \par \par \uc2 - A professor recalls how lifting the ban on the Muslim headscarf at Ankara University's theology faculty in 1988 quickly led to all women students wearing the garment. She says she fears similar social pressure on uncovered women if the government lifts the headscarf ban now, as it hopes to do.   \par \par \uc2 RADIKAL   \par \par \uc2 - Under the planned new constitution, Turkey's ruling AK Party will reduce the powers of institutions that have opposed its rule over the past few years, such as the board supervising universities and the Constitutional Court.   \par \par \uc2 MILLIYET   \par \par \uc2 - AK Party members are upset by plans to make religious education in schools optional. The AK Party has Islamist roots and its rank-and-file are conservative and religious-minded.   \par \par \uc2 REFERANS   \par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the dollar weakens below the 1.20 level to the lira. The lira is now at 1.23. The paper also says Turkish interest rates could be cut by 200 basis points by end-2007.   \par \par \uc2 CUMHURIYET   \par \par \uc2 - Turkish university rectors defend their right to comment on and criticise government plans to overhaul the constitution, saying their duty is to enlighten the public. Prime Minister Tayyip Erdogan had blasted the rectors over their criticism, telling them to "mind their own business".   \par \par \uc2 ZAMAN   \par \par \uc2 - Architects of Turkey's present constitution, introduced in 1982 after a military coup, back plans for a new "civilian constitution" that boosts individual rights and freedoms.   \par \par \uc2 VATAN   \par \par \uc2 - Prime Minister Tayyip Erdogan will lobby other leaders on behalf of Turkey's bid to become a temporary member of the U.N. Security Council when he attends the annual general assembly in New York. He is due to meet 10 presidents, 30 prime ministers and 50 foreign ministers in the coming days.   \par \par \uc2 YENI SAFAK   \par \par \uc2 - The head of the state body overseeing religious activity in Turkey, the 'Diyanet', says religious instrucution must remain compulsory in schools. Making it optional would fan divisions between different sects, he says.   \par \par \uc2 TURKEY-PRESS/|LANGEN|MD|G|RBN|REVU|RNP|DNP|PGE|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070921e39l000xj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
