\par\par \b \uc2 Turkish assets unfazed by Gul's talk on presidency   \b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2007\par 16:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, July 25 (Reuters) - The Turkish lira weakened slightly on Wednesday along with other Turkish assets although comments by Foreign Minister Abdullah Gul that he may make a fresh bid for Turkey's presidency elicited little reaction.   \par \par \uc2 Turkey's secularists including army generals blocked Gul's first attempt to be elected head of state in May, forcing last Sunday's early parliamentary election, which resulted in a decisive victory for the pro-business AK Party.   \par \par \uc2 That win prompted the lira shot to a 6-year high and the stock exchange rallied, although profit-taking has since crimped gains slightly.   \par \par \uc2 The lira <IYIX=> weakened 0.6 percent to close at 1.2420 on the interbank market on Wednesday after it rallied 1.4 percent to close at 1.2340 on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 The main stock index <.XU100>, which surged 5.1 percent to a record close on Monday, closed 0.2 percent lower at 54,832 points on Wednesday amid profit-talking.   \par \par \uc2 "Gul's comments didn't affect the market, although we are monitoring the presidential elections. Global markets are slightly more important for us as we wait for a clearer picture on the elections," said a bond trader.   \par \par \uc2 Markets have been boosted by the AK Party winning 47 percent of the vote in Sunday's general election, allowing it to govern alone again and press ahead with a reformist agenda.   \par \par \uc2 However, the party must now solve the row over a successor to President Ahmet Necdet Sezer, whose term expired in May, and which has pitted secularists against the AK Party.   \par \par \uc2 Separately on Wednesday, bids at Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  daily dollar-buying auction were $288 million, more than seven times a maximum buying limit of $40 million, at the first auction since it raised the buying limit.   \par \par \uc2 The bank bought $40 million, the full amount, at a maximum price of 1.2475 lira <IYIX=>, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed.   \par \par \uc2 Raymond James Chief Economist Ozgur Altug said bid volume was the second highest since 2002.   \par \par \uc2 "This shows that there is plenty of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply, which will exert pressure on the exchange rate in the coming period. If this trend continues, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Bonds eased with the yield on the May 6, 2009 benchmark bond <0#TRTSYSUM=IS> rising to 17.26 percent from Tuesday's 17.18 percent.   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-TURKEY|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|MD|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070725e37p001wx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
