\par\par \b \uc2 FOCUS: Stakes High As Turkey Goes To The Polls   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Emsden   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,280 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2007\par 11:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MILAN (Dow Jones--With just a few days to go before Turkey's enigmatic July 22 elections, Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan has upped the ante by pledging to quit politics if his AK Party doesn't emerge with an electoral mandate to form a single-party government.   \par \par \uc2 Opinion polls and analysts reckon he'll be proven right and that the AKP, which stands for Justice and Development, can remain solely at the helm and steer through reforms to keep fueling the 7% annual economic growth rate of recent years.   \par \par \uc2 But the road ahead may be bumpier than it seems. A surprise electoral result could plunge Turkish markets into turmoil. Even the expected AKP victory will require delicate footwork to avoid confrontation with the military, which has openly accused the AKP of being a threat to secular institutions.   \par \par \uc2 Most of all, Turkey's next government will have to move beyond the acclaimed macroeconomic reforms of recent years to find more concrete ways of boosting productivity and employment, goals that will require policies destined to disturb notable swathes of society.   \par \par \uc2 "The tension is not about the regime, but arises from Turkey's structural transformation," says  \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  economist Serhan Cevik.   \par \par \uc2 In the past, he notes, the Turkish state owned firms and operated subsidy schemes to establish itself as the ultimate distributor of income and wealth. The AKP's more free-market approach has, since 2002, doubled per capita income and exports but has also threatened many perceived privileges.   \par \par \uc2 More will be needed, says Cevik, who hopes Turkey will adopt a "big bang" approach to reform after the vote.   \par \par \uc2 The AKP touts plans to liberalize labor markets, bolster ownership and contract laws, and overhaul the tax system.   \par \par \uc2 It is also keen to pursue large-scale privatization - toll highway concessions are already on the block and energy utilities are ripe to join them - with the aim of promoting greater productivity as well as further cutting state debt.   \par \par \uc2 Since the AKP took the helm, the Istanbul stock market has quadrupled, rising another 14% since June 26 alone and Turkish inflation has fallen to a near 35-year low. But its record on employment - a daunting challenge given that more than one million Turks join the workforce each year - is weaker: the jobless rate has fallen to only 9.8%% from 10.5% five years earlier.   \par \par \uc2 Job prospects are a frequent topic on the campaign trail, according to Mehmet Simsek, a former  \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  economist who is now an AKP candidate and tipped for an important economics portfolio in the next administration.   \par \par \uc2 Future job growth is likely to come from Turkey's burgeoning industrial sector - especially in automotive and electronics - which are replacing traditional sectors such as agriculture and textiles. But success in those new areas will require robust productivity gains and, if European experience is any indication, relative wage restraint.   \par \par \uc2 The economic policies offered by opposition parties appear aimed at tempting vulnerable voters.   \par \par \uc2 Cihan Pacaci, an Ankara candidate for the right-wing Nationalist Movement Party, or MHP, says the AKP has left a "wrecked economy" with inadequate job creation and too much reliance on foreign capital. He pledges to "implement a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy" to boost exports, and to privatize by local public share offerings rather than sell them to foreign investors.   \par \par \uc2 Algan Hacaloglu, deputy chairman of the left-leaning Republican People's Party, or CHP, says his party will make agriculture "a driving force in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 " and hints at more government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through an "industrialization policy" that singles out sectors ripe for public support.   \par \par \uc2 The mercurial Youth Party, led by controversial entrepreneur Cem Uzan, promises to halve the price of diesel fuel and double procurement prices for hazelnuts, a key product along the Black Sea coast.   \par \par \uc2 The AKP has indulged in some populism of its own, hiking hazelnut prices by seven times the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation target, delaying energy price hikes and vowing to slash sales taxes on tourism and agricultural enterprises. But it is the only party that appears to accept rules foreign capital will embrace.   \par \par \uc2 Turkey is a "good and improving credit story" but it "gets tricky" if the AKP doesn't stay in power, says Tim Ash, an analyst for  \uc2 Bear Stearns.\uc2  He notes the AKP is talking up plans to lift the country's credit rating up three notches to investment grade by the end of 2009.   \par \par \uc2 Recent polls suggest the AKP is on course to win around 41% of the vote, the CHP 18%, MHP 12%, with the Youth Party polling between 6%-10%. Independent Kurdish candidates, mostly from southeastern Turkey, are also expected to win at least 30 seats in Turkey's 550-seat assembly.   \par \par \uc2 The final numbers will be particularly crucial because parties that win less than 10% of the popular vote aren't assigned any parliamentary seats.   \par \par \uc2 That stringent threshold explains why, in the 2002 vote, the AKP won a whopping 365 seats - giving it a powerful majority - on the back of just 34% of the vote. Only the CHP won more than 10% of the vote.   \par \par \uc2 If both the MHP and the Youth Party pass the threshold this time, the AKP will probably not be able to form a government, unless perhaps by inviting the Kurdish deputies into a coalition.   \par \par \uc2 But the prospect of an "Islamic/Kurdish" government ruling for five years "would be a recipe for serious political crisis and possible military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," warns  \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  analyst Ahmet Akarli.   \par \par \uc2 If only the CHP and MHP enter parliament, the AKP will have to win more than 36% of the vote or risk having a majority so slim as to exacerbate factional infighting inside the party, Akarli said.   \par \par \uc2 Erdogan "will need a tighter but leaner party in his second term," he added.   \par \par \uc2 Anticipating such a need, the prime minister has replaced more than 150 current AKP deputies with new candidates, often shedding Islamic hard-liners with centrist technocrats such as Simsek.   \par \par \uc2 Traditionally, Turkish voters tend to turn against incumbent parties, but also reward recent economic performance, and historical patterns suggest the AKP will win 40% of the vote, says says Ali Akarca, a professor of political science at universities in Chicago and Ankara who has carried out extensive research into electoral habits.   \par \par \uc2 An export surge helped Turkey's economy expand at a 6.8% annual rate in the first quarter, much faster than expected. But private final consumption - presumably what matters to voters - slowed to a meager 1.6%, suggesting swathes of discontent may emerge at the ballot box.   \par \par \uc2 Small-town merchants are particularly grumpy, as modern supermarket chains are poaching their clients and even the rebounding tourist flows offer little business as vacationers increasingly opt for all-inclusive package deals, Akarca said from his farm on the Aegean coast.   \par \par \uc2 Markets are priced for the benign scenario in which the AKP retains power on its own but doesn't muster a two-thirds majority enabling it to change the constitution. The odds of such an outcome are 60% to 80%, according to analysts.   \par \par \uc2 Investors should be wary as that leaves plenty of room for "icebergs in the Mediterranean," says Ali Al-Eyd, emerging markets specialist for Dresdner Kleinwort, who is underweight on Turkish government debt.   \par \par \uc2 Any outcome other than the "Goldilocks" scenario of a modest AKP victory would likely produce an impasse preventing the new parliament from appointing a new president in the autumn and requiring new elections.   \par \par \uc2 -By Christopher Emsden, Dow Jones Newswires; +39-02-58-21-99-05; chris.emsden@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 (Clare Connaghan in London contributed to this item) [ 19-07-07 0916GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 5008   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070719e37j000xp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
