\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging debt-Market drifts higher in illiquid trade, Turkey up  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 570 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2006\par 14:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 29 (Reuters) - Emerging sovereign debt prices rose modestly in quiet trade on Thursday as investors have little impetus to trade in the illiquid conditions ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve's interest rates decision.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark JP Morgan Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus (EMBI+) <11EMJ> <.JPMEMBIPLUS> showed yield spreads narrowed by one basis point to 228 basis points over slightly stronger U.S. Treasuries.  \par \par \uc2 Overall, investors dipping into the market found illiquid conditions ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve's interest rate decision, widely expected to produce the 17th straight quarter percentage point increase to 5.25 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Trade in Turkey's sovereign bonds was light but the credit was showing signs of a slight correction higher.  \par \par \uc2 Turkey's debt has come under pressure as a result of the heavy sell-off in the lira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Rising inflation and tightening global monetary policies have conspired against Turkish assets.  \par \par \uc2 Until this week's concerted efforts by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates, drain lira from the financial system, sell dollars and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the market, investors were losing faith in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's much improved economy.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 2030 bond <TRGLB30=RR> on Thursday rose 0.25 points in price to bid 134.00, yielding 8.508 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Investors have bought the credit back after pushing prices down to 15 months lows earlier this week of 130.75, which in turn pushed the yield up to 8.736 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "The Turkey 30s have bounced three points from the lows, but this is only a blip compared with the losses of the past three months. Thus, the dead cat bounce, short covering, relief rally, could continue a little while longer," Richard Sega, portfolio strategist at Argonaftis Capital in London wrote to clients.  \par \par \uc2 "Balanced against this, though, nerves ahead of the inflation data, plentiful broker recommendations to short the 30s against the 34s, 14s and 15s, etc, will limit the gains," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Turkey's five-year credit default swaps -- insurance like products that protect against default or credit restructurings -- narrowed by about 20 basis points to 300 basis points on Thursday <TURKEY5UA=GFI>.  \par \par \uc2 While narrowing spreads show a reduction in risk aversion, spreads are just off their widest point in 13 months.  \par \par \uc2 "We're seeing short-covering off the street after two weeks of permanently softer days, so this is kind of a bounce which started yesterday but I would not characterize it as aggressive client buying," said one Turkish debt trader at a German bank in London.  \par \par \uc2 "People are looking at the bonds and maybe thinking they are cheap. It is a corrective trade, but we are really waiting to see what Turkey's inflation figures show next week and we have the Fed tonight, so that is keeping a lid on turnover," the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Brazil's benchmark 2040 bond <BRGLB40=RR> rose 0.187 points in price to bid 121.875, yielding 7.624 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings upgraded Brazil's credit rating late on Wednesday to 'BB' from 'BB-', citing ongoing improvement in external finances despite the turbulence in emerging markets.  \par \par \uc2 Russia's benchmark sovereign bond due 2030 <RUSGLB30=RR> rose 0.125 points in price to bid 105.438, yielding 6.521 percent.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING-DEBT|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par