\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Turkish lira hits 3-yr low before rate decision  \b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2006\par 12:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, June 20 (Reuters) - The Turkish lira <IYIX=> sank on Tuesday to its weakest level against the dollar since April 2003 and bond yields soared amid domestic political concerns and rising interest rates in the big global economies.  \par \par \uc2 Markets were especially jittery as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy committee was due to announce later in the day whether it was raising interest rates further after last week's 175 basis point hike. Most analysts expect no change for now.  \par \par \uc2 The lira traded at 1.6275 to the greenback on the interbank market in Wednesday-dated trade, down from a closing level of 1.6170. It has lost more than 18 percent of its value since the end of April.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when the lira last approached those levels against the dollar a week ago. Bankers estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was worth $150 million. \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  has some $60 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on the benchmark April 9, 2008 bond <0#TRTSYSUM=IS> ended on Tuesday at 19.90 percent, the highest since January 2005 and up from Monday's 19.73 percent. The main Istanbul share index <.XU100> closed down 0.59 percent at 34,601.40 points.  \par \par \uc2 "I think foreign investors are suspicious about Turkey because of political concerns, because of oil (prices) and expectations of rate rises in the United States and Japan," said one banker.  \par \par \uc2 Increased expectations of continuing U.S. rate hikes are pulling cash out of riskier markets as higher U.S. rates change the risk-reward ratio. The U.S. Federal Reserve meets at the end of the month and is expected to tighten policy again.  \par \par \uc2 Investors have also been rattled by Turkey's war of words with the European Union over Cyprus, which threatens to derail Ankara's negotiations to join the EU.  \par \par \uc2 Ankara says it will not open its ports to Cypriot traffic until the EU lifts trade restrictions against breakaway Turkish Cypriots on the partitioned island -- a stance Prime Minister Tayyip Erdogan reaffirmed in parliament on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll showed Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not expected to alter its interest rates after Tuesday's meeting. But the bank has signalled it is ready to tighten credit further if this is deemed necessary to counter inflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-TURKEY (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|MD|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060620e26k000oa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
