\par\par \b \uc2 PRESS DIGEST - Turkey - June 19  \b0\par\par\uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 2006\par 08:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 ANKARA, June 19 (Reuters) - These are the leading stories in the Turkish press on Monday. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy.  \par \par \uc2 RADIKAL  \par \par \uc2 - The paper draws attention to the parlous state of Turkey's European Union General Secretariat, which is meant to steer the country's EU membership drive. It says the secretariat lacks sufficient staff and its building, on the outskirts of Ankara, even suffers shortages of basic utilities.  \par \par \uc2 MILLIYET  \par \par \uc2 - A Japanese firm building a tunnel between the European and Asian shores of Istanbul beneath the Bosphorus plans to lower the first of the huge tube-like compartments to the water bed in September. It will avoid using divers due to strong currents.  \par \par \uc2 - Turkish Cypriot leader Mehmet Ali Talat says Turkey must not secretly open its ports to rival Greek Cypriots. Ankara has so far resisted EU pressure to open its ports, saying the EU must first lift trade restrictions against the Turkish Cypriots.  \par \par \uc2 HURRIYET  \par \par \uc2 - Turkey's Supreme Court wants a seven-year jail sentence for a lawyer who made sexual advances to a secretary, the paper says. He was originally sentenced to six months.  \par \par \uc2 - Greece is preparing a new proposal to try to break the deadlock in Cyprus peace talks, according to a Greek newspaper.  \par \par \uc2 SABAH  \par \par \uc2 - Some cabinet ministers plan to block the appointment of Yasar Buyukanit as the next head of Turkey's military General Staff, the paper says. Buyukanit, currently head of land forces, is seen as more hawkishly secularist than incumbent Hilmi Ozkok, who is due to retire in August.  \par \par \uc2 CUMHURIYET  \par \par \uc2 - Billionaire philanthropist George Soros is in Istanbul to meet government ministers and to enlarge his business activities in Turkey, the paper says.  \par \par \uc2 - Austria, which holds the EU's rotating presidency, revives the idea of offering Turkey a "privileged partnership" that would fall well short of full membership.  \par \par \uc2 YENI SAFAK  \par \par \uc2 - Around 1.5 million young people sat university entrance exams on Sunday. Many found the section of mathematics and science especially difficult.  \par \par \uc2 - Turkish Airlines cancels some flights over the weekend due to a shortage of air hostesses but insists there is no problem and notches up a record number of passengers on Sunday.  \par \par \uc2 REFERANS  \par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the lira weakesn to 1.65 against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 DUNYA  \par \par \uc2 - Bankers expect a further hike in interest rates after June inflation data are published, the paper says. They also predict a rocky summer in Turkey even if the volatility in global markets subsides.  \par \par \uc2 PRESS-TURKEY|LANGEN|G|RBN|REVU|MD|RNP|DNP|PGE|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060619e26j000ax\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
