\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Turkey tries to boost lira amid inflation woes  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Humeyra Pamuk and Gamze Alarslan  \par \uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2006\par 12:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds U.S. Treasury official remarks)  \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, June 13 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the lira on Tuesday for the first time since May 2004, after a sharp rise in interest rates failed to halt its slide following a shock rise in inflation.  \par \par \uc2 In addition to global stock market jitters and expectations of rising U.S. interest rates, Turkish markets have also been volatile because of domestic political tensions and a large current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Bankers estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was worth $150 million, while analysts said it would have a limited impact.  \par \par \uc2 The lira lost 15 percent of its value in May -- which could fuel inflation -- and is trading at levels seen previously in April 2003, around the time of war in neighbouring Iraq.  \par \par \uc2 "Due to the international conditions we have observed extreme volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market...and in order to curb extreme volatility the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market directly," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement.  \par \par \uc2 The lira <IYIX=>, which had traded as weak as 1.6230 -- down some 4.6 percent from its previous interbank close -- strengthened after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to close at 1.6050. But that was still 3.5 percent weaker on the day.  \par \par \uc2 "They've barely stepped in ... I think they were trying to test the environment to see what kind of amounts they would need to put in," said Lars Christensen, economist at Danske Bank.  \par \par \uc2 He added a further rate rise now seemed more likely after a surprise 175 basis point hike this month, as it showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trying to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The bank, which targets 5 percent inflation in 2006, had flagged a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently, noting it had some $60 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 "I would be surprised if it worked," one dealer said, adding the lira could move back to 1.65.  \par \par \uc2 A senior U.S. Treasury official said Turkey had good economic policies and was suffering from a global liquidity squeeze despite sound economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 "They've got good policies in place," Tim Adams told reporters travelling with him. Adams is in Turkey for consultations with economic officials.  \par \par \uc2 "What we're seeing is that Turkey is getting caught up as are many countries around the world, in an era of global transition from benign economic conditions...to a set of conditions in which central banks are draining liquidity," Adams said.  \par \par \uc2 He also praised the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for raising interest rates a larger-than-expected 175 basis points.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. INFLATION  \par \par \uc2 Investors said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not take their eyes off U.S. inflation data due on Wednesday, which could argue for higher rates there. Higher U.S. rates pull funds out of emerging markets, by changing the risk-reward pay-off.  \par \par \uc2 "The Turkish market will need further clarity over U.S. rates ... to recover," Anne-Sophie Beotti, economist at BNP Paribas, said.  \par \par \uc2 The main Istanbul stock index <.XU100> fell 5.7 percent to 32,384 points on Tuesday, bringing its loss since a 2006 peak to around a third.  \par \par \uc2 Bond yields, sensitive to inflation and rates, soared to their highest level since January 2005 on Tuesday, with the benchmark bond trading as high as 19.3 percent. That compares with the current cost of funding of 16.2 percent and overnight benchmark borrowing rates of 15 percent.  \par \par \uc2 A Treasury auction on Tuesday saw weak demand and the third issue of the benchmark April 9, 2008 bond yielded a higher-than-expected 18.97 percent at the sale, and it yielded an average 18.82 percent on the day <0#TRTSYSUM=IS>.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed comments from rating agency Fitch on Tuesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was behind the curve responding to inflation -- which came in at 10 percent in May -- and said its large rate hike earlier this month "smacked a bit of panic".  \par \par \uc2 Governor Durmus Yilmaz earlier justified the bank's decisions, saying the rate hike had positive effects and there was no question the bank might have been late.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 TURKEY\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 4)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|MD|BNX|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060613e26d00132\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
