\par\par \b \uc2 US Tsy's Adams: World Adjusting To Tighter Liquidity  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Price  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2006\par 15:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Turkey's latest bout of financial markets turmoil reflects a global adjustment to tighter liquidity, the U.S. Treasury's top international policy adviser said Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Turkey has an experienced economic team with a "proven track record of good economic policy," Treasury Undersecretary for International Affairs Tim Adams told reporters traveling with him here.  \par \par \uc2 Adams, after stops in Russia and Estonia, is in Turkey meeting with local business leaders and economists.  \par \par \uc2 "We're in a global transition from an era of benign economic conditions and a tremendous amount of liquidity sloshing around the world to one in which there is less liquidity and central banks are draining some of that liquidity out of the system," Adams said.  \par \par \uc2 "But the underlying fundamentals here are pretty good," he added. [ 13-06-06 1417GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Adams said the shift in liquidity has created "excess volatility" in financial markets all over the world as well as in Turkey, adding that in his view, the current bout of financial markets volatility is a "transition period."  \par \par \uc2 Turkish financial markets, along with other developing economies, have been churning since mid-May after investors raised their expectations for higher inflation and interest rates in the U.S.  \par \par \uc2 The uncertainty has markets more preoccupied with risk and putting new scrutiny on countries' policies following a long period of financial stability and low interest rate spreads for emerging market borrowers.  \par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, International Monetary Fund Managing Director Rodrigo Rato, said developing countries are going to have to adapt to the new environment.  \par \par \uc2 "Probably the risk appraisal of the past couple of years has been extremely benign and not consistent with historical analysis of risk," Rato told reporters after participating in a G8 finance ministers meeting hosted by Russia last weekend. "The volatility in the financial markets is an issue which emerging economies have to be very aware...the markets, in this new appraisal of risk, are going to become more attentive to vulnerabilities," Rato said.  \par \par \uc2 Turkey, one of the hardest hit by the turmoil, saw its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciate from 1.32 against the U.S. dollar on May 8 to 1.6 against the dollar Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The lira's weakness came despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision earlier this month to raise interest rates by 175 basis points to 15% with the aim of keeping inflation in check.  \par \par \uc2 "You have a proven track record and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that took a bold move to ensure that it has the credibility of the markets and to maintain price stability," Adams said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday it sold U.S. dollars on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, in an attempt to avoid excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. Adams declined to comment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Adams said it won't be easy for \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to meet its inflation target as prices are influenced by high energy costs and the recent depreciation of the lira.  \par \par \uc2 "It will certainly challenge them to meet their targets in the short term," Adams said  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Tuesday, Fitch Ratings sovereign analyst Ed Parker said Turkey's positive rating outlook doesn't look as secure as did a few months ago.  \par \par \uc2 Parker said at a conference in London that the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to respond aggressively to curb inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Fitch expects Turkish inflation to creep up to 11% by the end of the year, falling to 8% by the end of 2007. He said the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive rate hike "smacked of panic," and a more gradual increase would have been viewed as a steadier solution.  \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings raised its outlook on Turkey's credit ratings to positive from stable in December, citing solid economic growth, a narrowing government deficit and debt burden and political stability. Turkey is rated BB- by both Fitch and Standard & Poor's Corp. and Ba3 by Moody's Investors Services.  \par \par \uc2 Adams said Turkey's program with the International Monetary Fund and its plans to join the European Union are important anchors for the economy.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF, which has a three-year $10 billion credit agreement with Turkey, praised the rate move, saying it showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is serious about battling inflation.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF executive board is expected to give final approval to the next two installments from Turkey's loan program in July, after the government has implemented some measures to ensure spending doesn't exceed budget targets. This would make about $2.0 billion of credit available to Turkey.  \par \par \uc2 Turkey concluded its first round of membership talks with the European Union on Monday, after E.U. foreign ministers reached a last-minute compromise to resolve a standoff with Cyprus over Ankara's relationship with the divided island.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par 