\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Turkish PM pledges no let-up in inflation fight  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Selcuk Gokoluk  \par \uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2006\par 11:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, brings in separates, adds details, background)  \par \par \uc2 ANKARA, May 16 (Reuters) - Prime Minister Tayyip Erdogan said on Tuesday Turkey would not make the slightest concession in its fight against inflation, seeking to ease concerns about the economy after a sharp sell-off in assets.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors' selling of the lira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bonds and shares accelerated in recent days following the publication on May 3 of worse-than-expected inflation for April, almost double the year-end target of five percent.  \par \par \uc2 "The basic priority is to reach the inflation target, we will not give the slightest concession from what we have achieved over inflation," Erdogan told a meeting of his Justice and Development Party (AKP) in parliament.  \par \par \uc2 Turkey has cut its inflation to single-digit levels under an International Monetary Fund-backed programme after decades of double and triple-digit inflation rates.  \par \par \uc2 But an 8.83 percent year-on-year rise in inflation in April helped push lira down by as much as 13 percent of its value against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last week and capital flight is estimated to have been around $4.5-5.5 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Factors behind the weakness also include a gaping current account deficit -- estimated to reach 6.3 percent of gross national product for 2006 -- political tensions and concerns that possible early elections could loosen spending discipline.  \par \par \uc2 Minister after minister has sought to ease concerns, stressing the government was committed to its current policies in what is turning out to be the among the most turbulent weeks for the ruling AKP since it came to power in 2002.  \par \par \uc2 "The upwards and downwards movements in the exchange rate should not be regarded as a source of problems," Economy Minister Ali Babacan said after a meeting with the banking sector in Istanbul on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "There will be no sacrifice from budget discipline or inflation as elections approach," he told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 EARLY ELECTION FEARS  \par \par \uc2 Fears of an early election and populist spending policies were also a trigger for the recent flight from Turkish assets.  \par \par \uc2 Erdogan, Turkey's most popular politician, dismissed speculation of early elections on Tuesday. Parliament must appoint a new president by May next year while legislative elections must be held by November 2007.  \par \par \uc2 "Neither elections nor populism or an election economy is part of our agenda," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Investors have been spooked by a standoff between the secularist President Ahmet Necdet Sezer and the AKP, which has roots in political Islam. Sezer who retires next year vetoed a key IMF-required social security reform bill last week.  \par \par \uc2 Turkey's powerful secularist establishment, which includes the military and the judiciary, are deeply suspicious of the AKP and fear the party will seek to loosen the strict separation between religion and the state if -- as expected -- the AKP picks the next president. The AKP denies this.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  under a recently appointed governor stepped into the ring on Monday, halting its dollar buying auctions under which bought up to $60 million a day.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts said if there were renewed lira weakness markets would expect more action than \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  or stop-gap monetary measures.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to re-anchor its policy framework with IMF support to weather the current storm. A purely monetary defence could prove ineffective, even counterproductive, in stabilising the lira," said analyst Ahmet Akarli at Goldman Sachs.  \par \par \uc2 Tera Brokers managing director Emre Tezmen said a rebound was likely after the sell-off of the last two weeks. And the lira rebounded slightly on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "But we recommend investors not to count on a sustainable bullish trend. Politics and international worries are likely to bring back the bears without delay," Tezmen said.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Humeyra Pamuk, Hidir Goktas and Daren Butler)  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-TURKEY-INFLATION (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|MD|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060516e25g000ta\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
