\par\par \b \uc2 Turk markets partially recover after capital flight  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daren Butler  \par \uc2 538 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2006\par 09:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, May 16 (Reuters) - The lira partially recovered  on Tuesday from Monday's 8 percent slide, dragging shares and bonds with it despite persistent worries about Turkey's economic and political outlook.  \par \par \uc2  The lira <IYIX=> firmed to 1.4600 against the dollar in early interbank trade. It closed at 1.4700 on Monday but spiked in after-hours trade to 1.5150. Last Friday it closed at 1.4005.  \par \par \uc2  "This movement is a correction of the (dollar's) rise over the last six days and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement may have had some impact. Whether it is lasting or not will depend on foreign trade," said Garanti Investment strategist Serdar Pazi.  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost as much as 13 percent of its value against the dollar over the last week and capital flight is estimated to be around $4.5 billion to $5.5 billion.  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took action on Monday, halting its dollar buying auctions under which bought up to $60 million a day.  \par \par \uc2  But analysts said if there were renewed lira weakness markets would expect more action than \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  or stop-gap monetary measures.  \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to re-anchor its policy framework with IMF support to weather the current storm. A purely monetary defence could prove ineffective, even counterproductive, in stabilising the lira," said analyst Ahmet Akarli from Goldman Sachs.  \par \par \uc2  Factors behind the market weakness include a wide current account deficit, high inflation, political tensions and concerns that possible early elections could loosen spending discipline.  \par \par \uc2  MODERATE REBOUND  \par \par \uc2  Higher inflation was reflected in a rise in the cost of borrowing, but bonds recovered partially on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2  The yield on the benchmark April 9, 2008 bond, which rose to its highest level of 2006 on Monday, fell 15.05 percent from a level of 15.16 percent in after-hours trading on Monday, but still sharply above Friday's level of 14.50 percent.  \par \par \uc2  Istanbul's main share index <.XU100>, which slid as much as 5 percent on Monday, rose 1.5 percent to 40,878.01.  \par \par \uc2  Tera Brokers managing director Emre Tezmen said a rebound was likely after the sell-off of the last fortnight.  \par \par \uc2  "But we recommend investors not to count on a sustainable bullish trend. Politics and international worries are likely to bring back the bears without delay," he said.  \par \par \uc2  Investors have also been unsettled by the impact of high oil prices on a hefty net importer, lower tourist numbers, fears of dwindling enthusiasm for EU-sought reforms and international tensions over neighbouring Iran's nuclear programme.  \par \par \uc2  Growing political tensions between Turkey's secular establishment and the ruling Justice and Development Party, which has Islamist roots, have also undermined sentiment. President Ahmet Necdet Sezer last week vetoed a key IMF-required reform law. The government plans to send the bill back to Sezer.  \par \par \uc2  A weaker lira will stoke inflation, already seen as one of the weak spots in the fast growing Turkish economy. Inflation in Turkey, which suffered a deep financial crisis in 2001, has only recently fallen to single digits.  (Additional reporting by Gamze Alarslan)      \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-TURKEY|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|MD|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060516e25g000g4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppic