\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Turk lira seen stronger, but fragility ahead  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Ross-Thomas  \par \uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2006\par 10:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, Feb 16 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lira is expected to defy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming months, rising against the dollar as it remains a favourite of investors, but it could weaken further out as risk appetite wanes and elections loom.  \par \par \uc2 After cries from exporters for a weaker lira <IYIX=>, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought an estimated $4.6 billion from the market on Wednesday, pushing the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down.  \par \par \uc2 But strategists said investors would take the opportunity to pour back into the market at the cheaper level, driving it back up.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be tested again," Deutsche Bank strategist Jens Nystedt said.  \par \par \uc2 While huge amounts of capital are flowing through global markets looking for returns and risk appetite is high, the particular appeals of the Turkish lira include a booming economy, expectations that rating agencies will upgrade Turkish assets and an attractive 13.5 percent interest rate.  \par \par \uc2 "Given its yield and the vast improvement in the economy I would expect to see (the lira) stronger," American Express economist Sarah Hewin said, adding she saw the lira at 1.25 to the dollar in three months, helped by dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 It closed at 1.3370 to the dollar on Wednesday but in early trade on Thursday had already crept up to 1.3325.  \par \par \uc2 Further down the line, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be so sought after.  \par \par \uc2 Turkey faces presidential and parliamentary elections in 2007 and some economists believe uncertainty over the fate of Prime Minister Tayyip Erdogan's reformist government could begin to erode the lira's value.  \par \par \uc2 "Over the past three years, the government has done everything on investors' wish list. As we go forward the risk of political noise or a less benign political backdrop increases," Merrill Lynch economist Mehmet Simsek said.  \par \par \uc2 Merrill's 12-month lira forecast is 1.42 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 GLOBAL LIQUIDITY, OIL  \par \par \uc2 Some economists also expect global liquidity gradually to dry up this year, which would hit the lira. If risk aversion increases, the gaping current account deficit -- not for now a problem to finance -- may become more of an issue for investors.  \par \par \uc2 The current account deficit ballooned 46 percent last year, swelled by high oil prices and surging imports.  \par \par \uc2 Since Turkey began targeting inflation formally in January, there has been speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less, particularly as it has said 2006 inflation was more likely to come in above its 5 percent target than below it.  \par \par \uc2 There is also some uncertainty over future policy as \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Sureyya Serdengecti's term expires in March.  \par \par \uc2 But several economists dismissed the notion that inflation targeting is incompatible with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Last year the Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  six times to buy dollars for lira, soaking up $14.6 billion.  \par \par \uc2 "I think there is this misperception in the market. Under the International Monetary Fund programme previously there were these targets for monetary expansion ... so last year actually the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was more constrained," CSFB economist Berna Beyazitoglu said.  \par \par \uc2 But there are reasons not to want to weaken the lira too much. The bank and the government recently discussed the lira's strength in the context of Turkey's gaping current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Turkey's other traditional problem is inflation, which tends to be checked by a stronger lira and the heavily indebted country benefits from a stronger lira when it comes to foreign debt payments.  \par \par \uc2 A more valuable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also makes oil cheaper, crucial in energy-dependent Turkey with oil prices around $60 a barrel and persistant geopolitical uncertainties.  \par \par \uc2 "They can't really tolerate too much lira weakness at the expense of weight on their oil bill," BNP Paribas economist Anne-Sophie Beotti said. "They are really vulnerable to a surge in oil prices."  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-TURKEY-LIRA (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|MD|BNX  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060216e22g000pd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
