\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Turkish shares close at record high on Vakifbank  \b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2005\par 15:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comment, bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closing)  \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, Nov 21 (Reuters) - Turkish shares closed at a new record high on Monday due to fresh capital inflow and the rise in banking sector shares after the initial public offering of Vakifbank shares last week.  \par \par \uc2 The main Istanbul share index <.XU100> closed up 0.96 percent at 35,654.99 points, beating the previous record closing level of 35,624.79 on Oct. 4.  \par \par \uc2 "Vakifbank IPO went very well and the serious interest confirmed that perspectives towards Turkey are still positive," said Tera Securities' Ayse Colak.  \par \par \uc2 The impact of strong demand in Vakifbank showed itself on Monday with the momentum in the buying in small- and medium-sized Turkish banks, said Colak.  \par \par \uc2 Vakifbank was the busiest share with 162 million lira ($118.89 million) as the banking shares index <.XBANK> closed 1.19 percent stronger.  \par \par \uc2 The IPO of some 25 percent of Vakif, Turkey's fifth largest bank, raised 1.74 billion lira, making it one of the largest emerging market banking listings this year.  \par \par \uc2 The lira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <IYIX=> stood at 1.3630 against the dollar in the spot market, compared with the previous 1.3650, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Bankers estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $3.2 billion to stem gains in lira after the start of its European Union membership talks.  \par \par \uc2 The new benchmark June 27, 2007 bond yielded 13.93 percent, compared with the previous 13.95 percent. The market is awaiting two debt auctions scheduled for Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The Treasury will hold two debt auctions on Tuesday, re-issuing a 315-day lira-denominated bill, which will mature on Oct. 4, 2006.  \par \par \uc2 It will also re-issue a 581-day lira-denominated bond on the same date, maturing on June 27, 2007. Both issues will be value-dated Nov. 23.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $45 million in its daily dollar auctions at an average 1.3657 dollar/lira exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 The bank has withdrawn a total $21.7 billion from the markets through six \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and daily auctions since Dec. 22, 2004.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 TURKEY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|MD|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051121e1bl001cy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
