\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Turkey  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  buys dollars to stem lira gains  \b0\par\par\uc2 173 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2005\par 12:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, background)  \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, Oct 4 (Reuters) - The Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, buying dollars for lira to stem gains in Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the start of its European Union membership talks.  \par \par \uc2 Bankers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 1.3355 against the dollar. At 1:45 pm (1045 GMT), best bids on the lira stood at 1.3520 on the interbank market.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on the buy side today to prevent existing and expected volatility under the influence of the start of the European Union accession procession," the bank said in a statement.  \par \par \uc2 All Turkish financial markets rallied on Tuesday morning on the back of the start of EU talks which fuelled investor confidence after two days of wrangling over Austrian and Turkish objections to the negotiating mandate.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 TURKEY\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|G|RBN|RNP|PGE  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051004e1a4000pq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
