\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1- \highlight22\uc2 Turkey \highlight\uc2  cuts rates,  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  on surging lira.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gill Tudor   \par \uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 17:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ANKARA, March 9 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates on Wednesday and bought dollars to curb the surging new lira, reflecting falling inflation and buoyant investor demand fuelled by the country's economic renaissance.   \par \par \uc2 Domestic bond yields are tempting foreign buyers, with interest also boosted by Turkey's strong economic rebound from a 2001 financial crisis and expectations it will start EU entry talks as planned on Oct. 3.   \par \par \uc2 In a bolder cut than some had expected, the bank reduced the overnight borrowing rate to 15.5 percent from 16.5 percent, and the overnight lending rate to 19.5 percent from 20.5 percent.   \par \par \uc2 The bank last cut rates on Feb. 9, by 50 basis points, and markets had largely priced in a further cut after February price data confirmed inflation was still on a downward trend.   \par \par \uc2 But with the new lira creeping towards four-year highs at just over 1.25 to the dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars and nudge the Turkish unit back beyond 1.27.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the two moves looked more like a chain of events than a co-ordinated campaign to halt the new lira's seemingly inexorable rise against the dollar, which puts ever greater pressure on Turkey's widening current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2 "They cut rates, some investors took their money, then others came in from outside, which drove the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even stronger," said Gregory Kronsten of WestLB in London. "Then the bank stepped in because the lira went too far in one day."   \par \par \uc2 The strong new lira helps Turkey service its substantial dollar-denominated debt, but has contributed to large trade and current account gaps as it feeds appetite for imports.   \par \par \uc2 Local bankers estimated the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at around $1.5-2.0 billion.   \par \par \uc2 "(This) will increase the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, thus providing for a further calming of any worries on the external balance," said Simon Quijano-Evans of Bank Austria Creditanstalt.   \par \par \uc2 INVESTOR DEMAND   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it entered the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb "excessive volatility" although the new lira has been firming more or less steadily since last September, when it was around 1.5 to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Kronsten said while much of this was due to the weak dollar, which has boosted emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the board, it also reflected foreign interest in Turkey's domestic bonds.   \par \par \uc2 "Turkey is a huge success story for offshore investors," he said. "Nowhere in the European time zone offers the same yield and a liquid market."   \par \par \uc2 Yields on the benchmark Sept. 27, 2006, bonds were at 15.91 percent on Wednesday afternoon compared with Tuesday's 16.13 percent. The new lira remained around 1.27 to the dollar while stocks were barely changed on the day.   \par \par \uc2 Inflation has fallen to single digits for the first time in a generation and Wednesday's interest rate move had been widely expected after February inflation data last week showed consumer prices rose by just 0.02 percent month-on-month - well on course for the government's year-end target of 8 percent.   \par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sounded a mild warning note to the government after reform delays that have held up Turkey's new $10 billion standby deal with the International Monetary Fund.   \par \par \uc2 Economy Minister Ali Babacan said on Wednesday a letter of intent on the new deal would be signed in the first half of April, but some analysts still saw this as disappointingly late.   \par \par \uc2 "Fiscal discipline and structural reforms are essential for maintaining the fall in inflation in the medium term and establishing permanent macroeconomic stability," the bank said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050309e13900b9z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
