\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Turkish c.bank buys dollars for lira - bankers.   \b0\par\par\uc2 258 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2005\par 11:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, Jan 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy dollars for lira on Thursday to curb volatility in the exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 The lira has reached its highest levels since April amid upbeat sentiment about the Turkish economy.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the first time the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars for lira since Feb. 16, 2004. The new lira weakened to 1.3380 against the dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from 1.3215 beforehand.   \par \par \uc2 "In recent days, there has been extreme movement in the exchange rates in connection with an increased supply of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement.   \par \par \uc2 "For this reason, a direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the form of buying was made today in order to prevent the exchange-rate volatility that has been observed.   \par \par \uc2 "This \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not express discomfort with the exchange rate level," the bank added.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not say how much it purchased, but bankers estimated the bank purchased between $150 million and $200 million in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Demand for Turkish assets has increased since the government won a date last month from the European Union to begin membership talks this year.   \par \par \uc2 Markets have also welcomed Turkey's new $10 billion stand-by agreement with the International Monetary Fund, which succeeds the current pact that expires next month.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts have said strong demand from foreign investors for high-yielding Turkish debt in recent weeks has also boosted the lira.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050127e11r0099m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
