\par\par \b \uc2 Turk mkts set to open flat after lira  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .   \b0\par\par\uc2 317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2003\par 08:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, Sept 26 (Reuters) - Turkish markets were expected to open flat on Friday, watching for statements on Turkey's $16 billion IMF pact amid a visit by a Fund team, brokers said.   \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday to buy dollars for lira pushed the lira back from year highs, but equities and debt rose as investors remained upbeat about the country's economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly stepped in to buy dollars as the lira hit levels around 1,350,000 to the dollar from Wednesday's close of 1,353,000. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished Thursday at 1,374,000 on the interbank market .   \par \par \uc2 The bank may have bought up to $1.5 billion for lira during the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Turkish press said on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 "Right now the optimistic atmosphere continues and sales on the stock market should be met by buying," said Pelin Okul at Koc Invest in Istanbul.   \par \par \uc2 The lira's rally from record lows of some 1,750,000 in March threatens Turkish exports, the engine behind economic growth after a deep recession in 2001, and a year-end target of $7.4 billion for the current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2 An IMF team arrived in Turkey this week for a latest review of the economic programme, linked to the release of a latest loan tranche.   \par \par \uc2 Benchmark August 18, 2004, bonds finished Thursday at 34.27 percent from Wednesday's 34.5 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Shares on the Istanbul bourse's main index closed Thursday one percent higher at 13,935.67 points.   \par \par \uc2 Okul said the stock market was likely to trade in a band between 13,700 and 14,200 points.   \par \par \uc2 Brokers said investors would also be watching for the release of an EU report on Turkey's progress on the political reforms needed to begin accession talks with Brussels.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030926dz9q001c2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
