\par\par \b \uc2  Turk cenbank says buys $2.1 bln for lira in May-June. \b0\par\par\uc2 245 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2003\par 15:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ANKARA, June 30 (Reuters) - Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it purchased $2.115 billion in U.S. dollars during May and June to arrest volatility in the lira's  value and to build up its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \par \par \uc2  The lira has firmed some 20 percent to around 1,415,000 against the U.S. dollar from a record low of around 1,770,000 in March, raising fears for the competitiveness of Turkey's exports and the current account. \par \par \uc2  The bank said it aimed to purchase up to $880 million in July during daily dollar purchase auctions of $40 million. It said it bought $566 million in dollars for lira on June 9 during an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  is looking to exports to help it meet a five percent gross national product (GNP) growth target for 2003, set out under a $16 billion IMF pact. \par \par \uc2  The ruling Justice and Development Party (AKP) and many businessmen have criticised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for not doing enough to stem the lira's gains against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at $28.132 billion on June 20 compared with $26.725 billion at the end of 2002. \par \par \uc2  Turkey on Monday reported a 7.4 percent rise in GNP during the first quarter of 2003 against expectations of a 6.2 percent increase. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030630dz6u00rx