\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Turk lira slips on c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 , stocks climb. \b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2003\par 16:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, May 21 (Reuters) - The Turkish lira fell back on Wednesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hovered at 11-month highs. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in as the lira traded at around 1,452,000 to the dollar on the interbank market . It was closed at 1,489,000, but was quoted at 1,503,000 in Thursday-dated trade. \par \par \uc2  The lira has rallied to levels not seen since last June with the swift end of the war in neighbouring Iraq. Investors had sent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 1,750,000 in March, fearing a protracted conflict next door would hammer \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's frail economy. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  today \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the buying side on seeing a repeat of extreme volatility," the bank said. \par \par \uc2  It said in a statement the lira's strength did not stem from "fundamental economic variables" but from Turkish corporate tax payments that are due and from a delay in the demand for dollars, possibly from importers. \par \par \uc2  The lira's slippage appeared to cheer stockbrokers and the main Istanbul index  climbed 1.09 percent to 10,540.85 points after weeks of losses or limited gains that many traders have blamed on the strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "With the overvalued lira, foreign investors have not seen sizeable returns (in dollar terms) from Turkish equities," said Hakan Avci, strategist at Global Securities. \par \par \uc2  Yields on March 26, 2004, debt firmed to 52.12 percent from a previous 53.5 percent, but bankers said the gains were limited as investors wait for International Monetary Fund inspectors to arrive for a crucial review of the $16 billion rescue pact. \par \par \uc2  "The downward trend in interest rates has been slowed ahead of the talks with the IMF and because the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still hasn't lowered its lending rates," one banker said. \par \par \uc2  The government's slow progress on key reforms it has promised the fund has raised questions about its commitment to the pact. The latest loan tranche of $500 million will only come after the successful completion of the review, the IMF has said. \par \par \uc2  The last review of the pact was delayed for months by a general election, little action on reforms and a lack of agreement on IMF demands for a cost-cutting 2003 budget. \par \par \uc2  Speculation mounted earlier this month the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cut its key overnight rates with signs that inflation was inching downward. But a rate cut now appears unlikely until at least after the IMF review. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030521dz5l00rxs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
