trategist at Global Securities. \par \par \uc2  Yields on March 26, 2004, debt firmed to 52.12 percent from a previous 53.5 percent, but bankers said the gains were limited as investors wait for International Monetary Fund inspectors to arrive for a crucial review of the $16 billion rescue pact. \par \par \uc2  "The downward trend in interest rates has been slowed ahead of the talks with the IMF and because the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still hasn't lowered its lending rates," one banker said. \par \par \uc2  The government's slow progress on key reforms it has promised the fund has raised questions about its commitment to the pact. The latest loan tranche of $500 million will only come after the successful completion of the review, the IMF has said. \par \par \uc2  The last review of the pact was delayed for months by a general election, little action on reforms and a lack of agreement on IMF demands for a cost-cutting 2003 budget. \par \par \uc2  Speculation mounted earlier this month the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cut its key overnight rates with signs that inflation was inching downward. But a rate cut now appears unlikely until at least after the IMF review. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030521dz5l00qjl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
