\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Fitch may revise Turkey outlook after IMF review. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Asli Kandemir \par \uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2003\par 16:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ANKARA, May 12 (Reuters) - International ratings agency Fitch said on Monday it may upgrade Turkey's country rating outlook to stable from negative following the next review of the country's $16 billion IMF pact, due to begin on May 21. \par \par \uc2  Fitch saw no hitches in Turkey tackling a domestic debt load of around $100 billion during 2003 but the heavily indebted country may experience some "stress" in 2004, Fitch analyst Nick Eisinger told Reuters. The agency's worries focused mainly on the budget and fiscal side, he added. \par \par \uc2  "If we see there is a good review without any major problems and we see that the (debt) auctions are continuing to the good and the fiscal numbers above all, we probably would take off the negative outlook," he said. \par \par \uc2  "We are probably looking at over the next four to six weeks." \par \par \uc2  Turkish markets have firmed recently after war in neighbouring Iraq ended. Investors are now looking to the IMF review, key to the release of a $500 million loan for Turkey's frail economy which is tentatively recovering from a deep slump. \par \par \uc2  "Even though the rating is now at negative outlook we certainly don't anticipate any debt repayment problems this year...But we will be looking on 2004 as potential stress here," Eisinger said. \par \par \uc2  Turkey has yet to implement several economic reforms it pledged the IMF it would tackle in April. The fund has said it will press Turkey hard to deliver on its commitments during the May review. A previous review was held up for months due to slow progress on economic reform. \par \par \uc2  "Our concern remains mainly on the budget, on the fiscal side and the potential impact that a debt crisis could have eventually on the balance of payments, but it's certainly not what you would call a traditional balance of payments problem that is pretty strong," he said. \par \par \uc2  Turkey's 2003 budget, a focus of recent talks with the IMF, requires deep spending cuts and measures to raise revenue. The fund says Ankara must stick to its promises to allay fears in financial markets that it is not fully committed to the pact. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to arrest a rally in the lira against the dollar that analysts say could hit exports and lead to a widening of the balance of payments deficit. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030512dz5c00rh4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
